+//
+// JERKWAD: the connection handling in this file is rewritten. Doom's netcode
+// was written for UDP, where "the link" cannot go away; here the link is one
+// WebSocket through a relay, and it can. The original treated that as an
+// afterthought and it cost whole games:
+//
+//  * Reconnecting was a BLOCKING call made from inside SendPacket/RecvPacket
+//    (sleep a full second, poll, repeat up to six times) — i.e. it froze the
+//    game loop mid-frame for longer than the netcode's own timeout allowed.
+//  * A socket was only known to be dead if the browser said so. A half-open
+//    one (a NAT that forgot us, a network path that died without a FIN, a
+//    close handshake the far side never answered) looked "open" forever, so
+//    packets went into the void until the other players were timed out.
+//  * The receive queue held 64 packets, about 1.8 seconds of game traffic.
+//    One stalled frame overflowed it, and every packet that did not fit was
+//    dropped AND leaked.
+//
+// Now: the first connection still blocks (nothing else can happen until it is
+// up), but after that the socket is managed by a small non-blocking state
+// machine driven from the packet calls. A lost socket is reopened in the
+// background while the game keeps running; packets that have nowhere to go
+// are dropped, which Doom's own resend logic is built to absorb. Liveness is
+// measured against the RELAY (a ping it answers), not against the other
+// players, so a frozen peer is never mistaken for a dead link.
+
